Well, I've read your story.  Let's put it this way:  Once I had
discovered the website, on which the first 39 chapters are placed, I
decided to read one chapter every day.  So it would have taken about a
month to read it all...  On the first day, I read 5 chapters and decided
not to read more than that on the following days.  That would have made
it last a week...  I sticked with my resolution the next day, but I had
finished everything after four days in total, including the two chapters
that are only in the newsgroup so far...

Since I discovered the story in the newsgroup first (actually I happened
across Galanda before Ellora and remembering only the name of her dog, I
searched for Ellora later to continue reading Galanda's story... this
way I met the *real* Ellora), I believed initially that this was really
your first game.  And I wondered how anyone not having played before
could get that far.  But even after I realized that the number of your
played games is probably even slightly larger than mine (though my total
playing time must well exceed yours - about 30 of my games were
ascensions, and those last much longer than games in which you get
yourself killed) I thought you were playing amazingly well for somebody
who refuses to get spoiled.  I would certainly not have done as well.

The difference is probably that I never played AD&D.  Apart from a month
of frustrating Rogue games, NH (then 3.1.3) was my first (and till now
only) roguelike game.  My approach was therefore completely different
from yours.  I discovered the game, had figured out how to move but did
not know anything about the rules of the game, just that I seemed to get
beaten up everytime I tried to proceed.  So I did a search in the (then
still young) internet and came across the Waterman spoilers.  There was
a warning in the beginning that on being spoiled the game would never be
the same for me but my horizon would incredibly widen.  This was true...
In fact, I knew I would never have the time to figure out things by
myself - I did not even know about a guidebook. May not have heard about
it before my first ascension (but I may err here).

So I must have been spoiled from my second or third game on.  Then I
played four games in "explore" mode, or rather, cheat mode.  That is, I
was savescumming.  For learning the game, I believe this is slightly
better than explore mode, because once you get more confident, you tend
to save more rarely, which means you have to be more careful not to die.
In explore mode, you can just pull through, as you can refuse to die
whenever you would normally.  When I died, I would kill the process
(this  was on a Unix computer) while the game still asked me questions,
so I would not enter the high-score record.  I played two of the four
games to a million score and the other two to ascension.  I don't know
how often I died, it could be 100 or 300 times, but then I knew many
possible deaths and had at least an idea how to avoid them.  When I was
beaten to death by a shop keeper (Asidonhopo?) because V unq qrfgeblrq
gur qbbe bs uvf fubc, I switched to explore mode in the saved copy and
fought him refusing to die until finally *he* died from my occasional
hits (must have beaten me dead at least ten times).  This way I came to
appreciate that a player is not a match for a shopkeeper at all in early
game.  I also managed once to upset all the watchmen in minetown and was
killed many times, before I got through. (On these occasions, I usually
went back to a copy before that point, since it was clear, I had done
something wrong.)

Well, after that I decided to try an ascension, and since I liked the
Indiana Jones movies, I started a series of archaeologists.  The fourth
or fifth one survived.  But he did not ascend.  Due to a bug of the
game, V pbhyq abg trg bar bs gur vgrzf lbh arrq gb trg gb gur cynpr
jurer gur Nzhyrg bs Lraqbe vf gb or sbhaq and so I just had to escape
the dungeon after 200000 moves.  The score was about 6 million, would
therefore have been 12 million, had he ascended.  I was terribly
frustrated and decided to do the easiest character class possible.  At
the time, there was a discussion in the newsgroup whether this was the
Barbarian or the Valkyrie.  Since I had already "ascended" a Valk (who
had made the acquaintance of a wand of death in her quest), I opted for
this one. Indeed, she ascended right away, with a score of about 8
million, after 150000 moves.  Later on, my games used to last s.th. like
100000 moves. I have never managed to speed through the dungeon like
some of the other posters that go to ascension in 60000 moves or less.
Since you are already beyond move 77000 with Ellora, I guess you will
not ascend before move 150000 either - if you do :-).  I would give you
a 50% chance (at maximum!) by now, considering what you know already and
what you don't. (For someone who knows the game well, it is more like
90% after the castle.)

Anyway, it is extremely interesting to follow the thinking process of
someone who is not spoiled while he is learning about the inner workings
of the game.  Makes me want to try out a similar thing with a variant
that I do not know yet, like Slash'em... to see what I can figure out
myself.  Then NH would be my AD&D equivalent.  Of course, NH and
Slash'em are much more closely related than AD&D and NH.  Still, I
suppose because dying at a late stage of the game due to something one
did not know is so incredibly frustrating and does not allow one to try
to rectify the situation, I would first do a game to the end in explore
mode.  I don't see what this takes away in terms of fun figuring out
things by myself.  The fun beating the game is a different kind of fun
(for me), and the learning time is so much shorter when you can go
through to the end, because you can actually find out what was your
error.  If you die in a regular game at turn 40000 due to some earlier
mistake you may not have a chance to figure out what was the problem,
even if you die the same way in two additional games after about the
same number of turns.  In explore mode, you will repeatedly die with
increasing frequency due to the same mistake while being able to
continue to observe...

Surprisingly, I even learned new things about the game from your
observations (which shows how rich a game it is).  You did not always
draw the right conclusions, but you were so careful in trying to observe
everything that might be important that you noticed details that were
new to me.  One thing is that you can confuse umber hulks with a mirror.
This is perfectly sensible, but I simply never tried, thinking the only
utility for a mirror is in confronting Medusa (and I know how it acts on
some "eryngvirf" bs ure, but this is not really useful in the game).
Another thing are the details that you found out about pricing - I never
took these painstaking efforts.  At some point you even fooled me into
believing I had overlooked something about ghosts and lit or unlit
temples.  You were so convinced that ghosts won't appear in a lit temple
and they did not for quite a while in your story...  I had never noticed
any difference, but I became unsure whether ghosts will really stay away
from lit temples.  As you have experienced yourself in the meantime,
they won't, and I *believe* that the lighting of the temple does not
make any difference at all.  (Tried it out in wizard mode, got three
ghosts in a sequence in a lit temple.)  But I am not hundred percent
certain after these observations of yours whether ghosts might not be
less probable in a lit qrfrpengrq temple than in an unlit one after all
- I am no source diver.  Finally, when you had pushed a boulder into a
moat that was part of a swamp and dug out stuff, I first thought this
was also something new to me.  Before, I thought I know all about
digging out stuff from former water areas...  In fact, it is not
difficult to get your experience into agreement with what I know, vs gur
zbng jnf bayl bar fdhner npebff, ohg vs vg jnf ovttre, V jbhyq or
crecyrkrq...

As you can see, I am trying to avoid mentioning things that you might
not yet have found out yourself, even if they would not be decisive for
the game in any way...  Actually, there was one point in the game when I
thought that someone should maybe spoil you in order to prevent instant
death.  That was after you fell from Medusa's level to the next one.
However, this was about chapter 20 and there were 39 of them.  So I knew
already you had survived although I did not know whether this was by
accident or whether you had had some information from outside the game.
As it turned out the latter was the case, the "deities from rgrn" had
tipped you off that the level above you was Medusa's.  I don't know
whether this minimal amount of spoiling bothered you at the time, but I
realized that it was actually unnecessary, since you would have survived
the encounter with Medusa anyway (univat guerr fbheprf bs ersyrpgvba,
bar bs juvpu lbh arire gbbx bss - gur nzhyrg).  Therefore, I think these
"deities" were not right in tipping you off.  Of course, I am myself
guilty of giving you some kind of hint at one point, which however was
nothing more than to encourage you that you might solve a certain
problem by thinking hard about it.  Had I known that this would lead
others to blurt out the solution, I would have held my tongue...  It
seems however that you got warned in time.  No offence meant!

To come back to the post I am referring to:  I have also sometimes been
extremely scared for Ellora.  She's so innocent!

